## 3. Methods

### 3.1 Validation-driven greedy elimination (our design)

Graph-based methods were implemented first and rejected *for this mechanism*: the DRGEP
interaction coefficient saturates (110 of 111 species reach R_A >= 1e-3, most exactly 1.0) because
the USC-II reaction graph is dense and third-body reactions connect everything, so the ranking has
no discriminative power (section 6).

Instead, candidate importance is **measured**:

1. probe set: 77 constant-volume adiabatic ignition cases (11 fuels x 7 T/P/phi/diluent states,
   all verified to ignite in the parent; tau from 4.2e-6 s to 0.52 s);
2. ranking pass: build the mechanism without each removable species (or reaction) and evaluate the
   probe-set ignition-delay error against the parent reference;
3. greedy batching: remove the smallest-error candidates first in batches; accept a batch only if
   the whole probe set stays inside a tolerance box (mean, 95th percentile and maximum of
   |ln(tau_reduced/tau_parent)|), otherwise halve the batch (bisection down to 1) and finally skip;
4. the family is emitted as the sequence of accepted steps, so the whole accuracy/complexity
   trade-off curve is available, not just one end point.

The probe set *is* the acceptance metric, so probe errors are **not** reported as accuracy; every
accuracy figure below comes from held-out conditions (section 4).

### 3.2 Mixture convention

All our cases use `X_fuel=1`, `X_O2=stoich_O2/phi`, `X_diluent=3.76*stoich_O2/phi`,
`stoich_O2 = C + H/4 - O/2` (`scripts/kinlib.py::mix_X`, which also maps fuel aliases such as
`nC4H10 -> C4H10`).  An earlier family was produced with the literal `X_O2=1/phi`, which makes
`phi=1` about `stoich_O2` times richer for C2+ fuels; that run is void and is reported as such
(sections 4.1, 6).

### 3.3 Observables and validation design

* **Ignition delay** - 0-D adiabatic constant-volume `IdealGasReactor` (rtol 1e-7, atol 1e-12),
  `tau` = time of maximum dT/dt, identical for the parent reference and for every candidate.
  Held-out matrix: 11 fuels x T{1000,1200,1450,1700} K x P{1,10,30} atm x phi{0.5,1.0,2.0} x
  {air, Ar} = 792 cases, deliberately offset from the probe states.  All 792 ignite in the parent
  (tau 6.9e-7 ... 0.768 s).
* **Laminar flame speed** - 1-D freely propagating flame (`ct.FreeFlame`, width 0.05 m,
  mixture-averaged transport, Soret off, staged solve with default refine criteria, Su = velocity
  at x = 0).  Reported set: 11 fuels at phi = 1, 300 K, 1 atm; the wider 45-case set
  (`data/flame_cases.csv`) adds phi = 0.7/1.3 and 5 atm / 500 K subsets.
  Equilibrium (HP) flame temperature is recorded per case.
* **Experimental comparison** - 289 shock-tube / RCM ignition-delay points over 5 fuels
  (`agents/expdata/data/exp_ignition.csv`, curated by the `expdata` researcher, with source URL,
  citation and digitisation flag per row; explicit initial compositions reconstructed and verified
  against the raw ReSpecTh XML, `scripts/exp_cases_from_expdata.py`), screened to
  `tau_exp <= 0.05 s` (section 4.4).  Laminar burning velocities from the same effort
  (`agents/expdata/data/exp_flamespeed.csv`) are compared with the computed flame speeds.
* **Literature baselines** - GRI-Mech 3.0 (53 species / 325 reactions) and DRM19 (21 / 84) on the
  same case sets, on the fuel subsets they contain.
* **Consistency** - element balance of every reaction, NASA7 cp/h/s continuity scan over
  300-3000 K, species/reaction counts, per-mechanism cost (wall time per case on the same machine
  and process count).
* Reporting tolerances (chosen, not inherited): |dln tau| > ln(1.35) = 0.30 is called a ignition
  "failure", |dSu/Su| > 15 % a flame failure.

### 3.4 Selection loop, and the flame-informed repair outside it

The greedy elimination loop (3.1) accepts a batch of species removals when the *ignition* probe set
stays inside the tolerance box; it never evaluates a flame.  Section 4.2 shows what that costs: the
members below ~70 species pass ignition and destroy the flame speed.  We therefore added an explicit
**second, post-hoc design step** to the workflow:

1. run the ignition-greedy family (parent 111 -> 93 -> 59 -> 50 species) and measure both observables
   for every member on the held-out matrix and the 11-fuel flame set;
2. when a member fails the flame criterion, re-add the parent species that the flame chemistry needs
   (`scripts/mk_backadd.py`, which rebuilds the mechanism from the parent text as
   `keep = species(member) + back-added species`, taking every parent reaction whose species all
   survive, and pruning removed species from third-body efficiency lists);
3. re-measure both observables, and keep the repaired member only if it passes both.

`results/red59_bA.yaml` (69 species) and `results/red50_bA.yaml` (63 species) are the products of
step 2/3.  This is *not* a flame-in-the-loop optimisation: the flame test is applied to a handful of
final candidates, not inside the ranking, and the back-added sets were chosen by chemical
reasoning (CH2/CH/C2H/HCCO/CH3CHO/CH3CO/CO2/propargyl-allene/C4H2 are the flame-critical block lost
between the 93- and 59-species steps) rather than by an automated sensitivity search.  Section 5
lists this as a limitation.

The same post-hoc pattern was used once more after the lean/high-pressure corner check of section
4.2.1: the allyl / propenyl-aldehyde block lost between the 93- and 63-species steps was re-added to
the compact members (`results/red50_bA_p6.yaml`, `results/red59_bA_p6.yaml`), which repairs the
lean-propene corner at both 1 and 5 atm at the cost of six species (section 4.2.3).  This second
repair is also outside the selection loop, and it moves the *measured* frontier rather than the
selection criterion: a flame-aware acceptance test inside the loop was still not affordable.

