## 5. Limitations

1. **Probe/validation mismatch in provenance.**  The delivered family was *selected* with a probe
   set whose mixtures used the wrong convention (section 3.2); its acceptance evidence is therefore
   void.  Its accuracy is instead measured on the correct-convention held-out matrix, flames and
   experimental points, which is what section 4 reports.  The corrected-convention re-run
   independently accepted 111 -> 103 -> 93 species with probe errors <= 1.2e-3 before it was killed
   by its 88.8-minute reservation (section 6), i.e. the conservative part of the family is
   corroborated by a valid probe, but the aggressive steps (-> 59, -> 50 species) are not.
2. **The selection loop is ignition-only; the flame test is outside it.**  Species removal was
   ranked and accepted on ignition delays only, and section 4.2 shows that this criterion does not
   transfer: members below ~70 species pass ignition and fail flames catastrophically.  The
   delivered 69- and 63-species members were repaired *afterwards* by re-adding a chemically chosen
   block of ten (resp. thirteen) parent species; the final flame check was applied to the repaired
   candidates, not inside the ranking, and no automated sensitivity search was used to choose the
   back-added set.  The reaction stage (section 4.2.2) has the same defect: its acceptance box is an
   ignition-probe box, and the mechanism it produced (69 sp / 415 rxn) passes that box while running
   14 % low on flames.  A proper fix - a flame-aware (or flame-surrogate) acceptance criterion inside
   the loop - was not affordable here: one flame evaluation costs ~0.5-10 minutes of wall time per
   case against ~0.02 s for one ignition case, i.e. 3-4 orders of magnitude, and the greedy loop
   evaluates of order 10^3 candidate batches.
3. **Observable definitions.**  Our ignition delay is a 0-D constant-volume max-dT/dt delay; shock
   tubes and RCMs define delays from pressure rise or OH*, which alone can differ by tens of
   percent.  Experimental comparison is therefore reported *relative to the parent's own
   deviation*.
4. **Flame validation is narrower than the ignition validation, and it has a hard corner.**  The
   member flame speeds are measured at 1 atm against the container parent matrix: 11 fuels at
   phi = 1, 300 K locally and the delivered 69/63-species members over the 66-case matrix
   11 fuels x phi {0.7, 1.0, 1.3} x T_in {300, 500} K; at 5 atm, the 93- and 69-species members
   over 11 fuels at phi = 1, 300 K, plus a targeted lean corner (C3H6 / C4H81 / C6H5CH3, phi = 0.7,
   300 K, all three members).  **That corner exposes a real limitation: the 69- and 63-species
   members under-predict lean C3H6 at 300 K / 5 atm by ~29 %, so they are qualified only for the
   tested 1-atm flame envelope (worst case ~23 %) and for phi = 1 at 5 atm; for low-temperature,
   high-pressure or lean conditions the 93-species member should be used.**  No member was
   validated above 5 atm.  The comparison against *experiment* is limited to C6H6/C6H5CH3
   (section 4.6), because no anonymous experimental laminar-flame-speed source was found for the
   other fuels (ReSpecTh's file endpoint returned HTTP 401).  Flame transport uses the parent's
   mixture-averaged parameters, unfitted, Soret off.
5. **No low-temperature chemistry validation.**  The probe and held-out matrix are at T >= 950 K
   (the matrix at T >= 1000 K).  USC-II has no low-temperature (NTC) chemistry, so this is not a
   loss for this mechanism, but nothing here validates 600-900 K behaviour or negative-temperature
   coefficient effects.
6. **Flame set is thin**: phi = 1 for 11 fuels at 300 K/1 atm locally, the delivered 69/63-species
   members over the 66-case 1-atm matrix (phi {0.7,1,1.3} x T_in {300,500} K), the 5-atm phi = 1
   subset and the lean 5-atm corner, plus the aromatic phi sweep; transport data (mixture-averaged,
   Soret off) is the parent's, unfitted.  Speciation-sensitive targets (soot, PAH, NOx, ignition
   behind reflected shocks at high dilution) were not measured.
7. **No speciation/soot/PAH validation**, and no species-by-species comparison was attempted; the
   task asks for macroscopic observables, and the removed species are exactly those whose
   concentrations are not measured here.
8. **Resource-limited**: one probe set, one greedy path, one tolerance box per stage; no global
   optimisation, no multi-start, so this is not evidence of optimality.

## 6. Failures and negatived results (honest log)

1. Cantera wheels installed outside the workspace do not persist in this sandbox; the workspace
   `pylibs/` target plus offline wheel extraction in the container fixed it.
2. The first remote job failed because queue inputs are copied preserving relative paths; `argv`
   must use the same relative path.
3. **DRGEP is useless on USC-II**: the interaction coefficient saturates (110/111 species >= 1e-3,
   most exactly 1.0) because of the dense graph and third-body hubs.  The reduction engine was
   redesigned around measured single-removal errors.
4. Third-body subtleties broke early candidate mechanisms: a species that appears only in
   third-body *efficiency* lists must not delete the reaction when removed, and Cantera parses
   e.g. `C6H5CH2OH + H <=> C6H5CHO + H2 + H` as a three-body reaction with the specific collider H,
   making `H`, `OH`, `HCO`, `H2`, `H2O`, `CO2` structural.  Both bugs were fixed and re-verified.
5. `rank_species()` originally removed all species except the candidate list, so the first ranking
   pass reported total failure for every candidate.
6. The flame driver first equilibrated the *cached* `Solution` in place, corrupting the unburned
   state (Su = 3.5 cm/s for CH4/air phi = 1); fixed - the corrected driver gives
   36.85 cm/s (literature 36-38 cm/s) and T_ad = 2230 K.
7. `nC4H10` is not a USC-II species name (it is `C4H10`); early case lists raised Cantera errors.
   Fuel aliases are now mapped centrally in `kinlib.canon_fuel`.
8. **Wrong mixture convention**: a first species-elimination run used `X_O2 = 1/phi`, i.e. every
   case ~`stoich_O2` times richer than labelled.  Its family is void (`results/void_fam_*.yaml`,
   registered as evidence `evidence_dc79b06c`) and is used only as an unvalidated fallback and as a
   comparison point; the corrected re-run replaced it.
9. The first flame job (harness, 66 cases) produced no data: `multiprocessing.TimeoutError` is not a
   subclass of the builtin, so the watchdog exception escaped, and every child re-extracted the
   wheels into the same directory, racing and corrupting imports.  Fixed (flat scheduler, parent
   extracts once, subprocess kill on timeout).
10. The harness ignition matrix (`agents/harness/parent_ign.csv`, 792 cases, 610 ok / 172 equil /
    10 noign) was built with the literal `X_O2 = 1/phi` convention and is therefore a *rich-shifted*
    stress set; it is not used as the primary reference.  Our own reference
    (`results/ref_ign_matrix.csv`, 792/792 ignite) is used instead.
11. **The corrected-convention species stage was killed by its reservation** after 88.8 min having
    accepted only two steps (111 -> 103 -> 93 species, probe mean |dln tau| = 1e-4 / 1.2e-3); the
    family files were not archived (only its stdout log).  Its ranking pass (108 candidates, 378 s)
    and the two accepted steps are the evidence that survives.  The affected mechanism family is
    therefore the corrected-convention prefix of 93 species, which we could not extend.
12. **Container entry point bug (two jobs lost in 6 s each):** `remote/validate_all.py` and
    `remote/full_validation.py` imported numpy *before* `remote_env.setup()` had extracted the
    offline wheels onto `sys.path`, so both died with `ModuleNotFoundError: numpy`; and a first
    attempt to run `scripts/local_flame_table.py` on the container used `python3 remote_env.py &&
    ...`, which only fixes the sys.path of the helper process.  Fixed with `remote/run_flame_table.py`
    (setup() in-process, then runpy) and by moving the numpy import after setup(); the wasted
    charge was 384 + 256 core-seconds.  The same trap fired once more in the final revision: a job
    that started with `python3 scripts/reduce_engine.py` died with `ModuleNotFoundError: numpy`
    (448 core-seconds), which is why `remote/run_reduce.py` now exists.
13. **Local background jobs die with their shell session** (the sandbox tears down the process group):
    the first local 4-mechanism flame table started with `nohup ... &` produced an empty file and no
    rows after 50 minutes.  Long local runs must be started inside a persistent session.
14. The reservation-based kill (6.11) was mitigated by adding `--deadline-s` to
    `scripts/reduce_engine.py::greedy()`: the greedy loop now stops cleanly at a wall-clock budget and
    still writes its mechanism and log, so a bounded job settles normally and its outputs are archived.
15. **Missing dependency in a job's input list**: the first pooled-validation job (7957382) died in
    4 seconds with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'fastmech'` because `scripts/fastmech.py`
    was not declared as an input; the reaction-stage job died twice (7957480, 7957380) - first
    because `fastmech.write_filtered()` asserted that the phase species list must change, which is
    false for reaction-only removals (fixed: the list is now allowed to be unchanged, with a
    quote-aware count guard instead of an assertion) and then because that guard counted quoted
    species names such as `'C5H5O(2,4)'` as several tokens (fixed with a quote-aware parser).
    Total wasted charge: ~1000 core-seconds.
17. **Reaction-network reduction passed ignition and failed flames** (section 4.2.2): the stage
    accepted 15 batches on the 77-case ignition probe (69 species / 522 -> 415 reactions) and stopped
    at its deadline, but the resulting file is 14.3 % (mean) / 25.6 % (worst) slow across all 11
    fuels on the held-out flame table, versus 2.9 % / 11.5 % for the 522-reaction member it was built
    from, while its held-out ignition error grew only from 0.060 to 0.092.  It is kept as an
    audit-trail artefact (`results/rxn_final_r1.yaml`) and is not delivered.  The run was also
    wasteful in one respect: the accepted intermediate states (512 / 486 / 470 reactions) were not
    declared as job outputs, so the only recoverable evidence of the smaller cuts is the log.
    A follow-up experiment protected the core radical pool (14 species, 27 eligible reactions) and
    the small radicals (8 species, 46 eligible) and reached only 497 and 482 reactions, with flame
    errors of 5.0 % and 5.1 % mean - both dominated by the delivered 63-species / 461-reaction
    member; they are kept as `results/rxn_prot{A,B}.yaml`.  One further job (7958131) was wasted on
    the numpy-before-setup trap (448 core-seconds, item 12).
18. 45 experimental points were excluded from the comparison for physical implausibility
    (section 4.4); the parent itself deviates from the remaining experimental points by a factor
    ~2.5 on average, which is reported as a property of the parent mechanism, not of the reduction.
19. **The compact members fail one corner of the flame envelope - found by a targeted check, not by
    the selection loop.**  The 66-case 1-atm matrix showed that the largest errors sit on the lean
    side; a 9-flame check at the opposite pressure corner (5 atm, 300 K, phi = 0.7,
    `results/red_5atm_lean.csv`, job 7958261) then showed that the 69- and 63-species members are
    ~29 % low on lean propene there, while the 93-species member is within 1 %.  This invalidated the
    earlier, too-optimistic sentence "the delivered members stay in the few-per-cent range at 5 atm
    as well" (the 11-case phi = 1 subset was not representative), so section 4.2.1 and limitation 4
    now state the qualified envelope explicitly.  Cost of finding it: 10 304 core-seconds.
20. **A validation job compared a mechanism against itself.**  The p6-repair job 7958474 was
    submitted with two mechanisms and no parent, and the driver uses the *first* mechanism as the
    flame reference when none is given: `red50_bA_p6` therefore came out with a flame error of
    exactly 0 % (self-comparison) and `red59_bA_p6` only relative to `red50_bA_p6`.  The ignition
    half of that job is valid (the ignition reference is a separate file), and the flame half was
    re-run as job 7958485 with the parent first in the list and all six mechanisms in one driver.
    Cost of the wasted half: 3 584 core-seconds; the mistake is the reason the p6 flame numbers in
    section 4.2.3 are quoted from job 7958485, not from 7958474.

